n a potassium channel permeate and occlude cation-selective pores</article-title><source>Neuron</source><year>2005</year><volume>45</volume><fpage>379</fpage><lpage>388</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15694325</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.neuron.2004.12.047</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sokolov</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Scheuer</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Catterall</surname><given-names>WA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ion permeation through a voltage-sensitive gating pore in brain sodium channels having voltage sensor mutations</article-title><source>Neuron</source><year>2005</year><volume>47</volume><fpage>183</fpage><lpage>189</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16039561</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.neuron.2005.06.012</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Starace</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name><name><surname>Bezanilla</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Histidine scanning mutagenesis of basic residues of the S4 segment of the shaker K+ channel</article-title><source>J Gen Physiol</source><year>2001</year><volume>117</volume><fpage>469</fpage><lpage>490</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11331357</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1085/jgp.117.5.469</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tombola</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Pathak</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name><name><surname>Gorostiza</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Isacoff</surname><given-names>EY</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The twisted ion-permeation pathway of a resting voltage-se